Subject: Read back the DexScreener 429 ring, so a rate-limit episode is diagnosable

The durable counters were always written (db.bumpDex429: `dex_429_total`,
`dex_429_at` and a 50-entry `dex_429_ring` of EPISODES — one per 90s backoff
window, not one per response) precisely so a burst could be diagnosed after
the fact: "are they clustered around a deploy, a feed storm, or a steady
drip?". Nothing ever read them back, so the 2026-09-21 profiles-feed episode
(one good profile list in 18 ticks for over an hour) could only be described
by the per-isolate `http429` counter that dies with its isolate.

`/debug/dex429` is read-only (three worker_state reads, no writes) and
answers both halves: the TOTAL for volume, the RING for shape, with
last-hour / 6-hour / 24-hour episode counts and every stamp as
ISO + "minutes ago" so the burst can be eyeballed against a deploy time.

--- a/src/worker.ts
+++ b/src/worker.ts
@@ -3683,12 +3683,67 @@
     if (url.pathname === "/debug/feed-stats") {
       try {
